Crisis

Parliament has been unable to pass a budget for 8 months. Healthcare wait times have doubled. A major corruption scandal has toppled two ministers. Economic growth has stalled. A new candidate proposes temporarily centralizing decisions to bypass the gridlock—faster decisions, but fewer checks

(Saikkonen & Christensen, 2022) — Guardians of Democracy or Passive Bystanders. Finland-specific conjoint on norm violations. Finds even in high-trust, low-polarization contexts like Finland, segments of voters will tolerate authoritarian leader behavior if it brings perceived benefits. Directly applicable. FOR THEM IS POLICY CONGRUENCE (IMMIGRATION POLICY). \parencite{Saikkonen2022}.

(Neundorf et al., 2025) — Elections Without Constraints (Neundorf et al., 2025) — 35,000-respondent conjoint across 32 countries showing citizens prioritize elections but care less about executive constraints, especially when prosperity is promised. \parencite[1]{Neundorf2026} ``citizens consistently prioritize free and fair elections, highlighting their salience as a core democratic value. However, executive constraints appear less central to citizens’ preferences, especially when set against the promise of economic prosperity.''

(Casiraghi et al., 2024) — Who Looks Up to the Leviathan (Casiraghi et al., 2024) — Conjoint on crisis types and state intervention. Shows ideology matters less than crisis type—governance crises (not security) shift support rightward even among centrists. ``the type of crisis matters marginally – right-wing respondents were more likely to support state interventions only in the case of terrorism.'' \parencite[317]{Casiraghi2024}

(Peters & Pierre, 2022) — Politicisation of the Public Service During Democratic Backsliding (Peters & Pierre, 2022) — Directly addresses mechanism: when civil service loses independence and expertise is marginalized, policy-making fails, citizens rationally shift toward strong leaders.

AJR: inverted u-curve: benevolent dictator in affluent autocracies (when inequality is low or when inequality is too high).


preferences have been found to put more weight on
strongly held policies than on democratic norms (Graham
and Svolik 2020).

# democracy question

I think we might have to devise a battery of questions (or a question) that is empirically based: too abstract but theoretically complete might not be understood by respondent. We might have to be pragmatic here.

``However, citizens are slightly less responsive to civil liberties (free speech versus absence of free speech) and much less responsive to horizontal accountability (constraints on the executive versus no constraints) than to economic well-being. Thus, responsiveness to checks and balances in particular is overshadowed by responsiveness to the economy, which we interpret as signaling that people prioritize the citizen-centered aspects of democracy (free speech and elections) to a larger extent than institutional aspects (executive constraints).''\parencite[14]{Neundorf2026}

``elections elicit stronger responses than civil liberties, and especially more than executive constraints'' \parencite[15]{Neundorf2026}